    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coventry, Rhode Island, takes its name from the English city of Coventry, a nod to the settlers’ heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s eastern edge is dotted with historic mill villages—Anthony, Arkwright, Blackrock, and others—each born in the 19th‑century industrial boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Historic landmarks like the 1740s Waterman Tavern and the 1770 Nathanael Greene Homestead still line Coventry’s streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cyclists and walkers enjoy the Coventry Greenway, a former railroad right‑of‑way now part of the East Coast Greenway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Coventry is Rhode Island’s largest town by land area, covering 59.5 square miles of mostly rural landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coventry%2C_Rhode_Island",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "During the Revolutionary War, Coventry’s residents backed the patriot cause, producing General Nathanael Greene, a key American army leader.",
